title: "Word Counter: Words, Characters, Reading Time and Limits"
layout: base.njk
date: 2026-11-07
updated: 2026-11-07
meta_title: "Word Counter: Words, Characters, Sentences and Reading Time"
meta_description: "Count words, characters with and without spaces, sentences and paragraphs as you type. See reading and speaking time, the words you repeat most, and how your text fits an X post, an SMS or a YouTube title. Nothing is uploaded."
meta_keywords: "word counter, character counter, word count online, character count with spaces, reading time calculator, sms character counter, tweet character counter, words per minute"
noIconFont: true
faqs:
  - q: "How does this counter decide what a word is?"
    a: "A word is any run of characters between spaces that contains at least one letter or digit. That is the rule Microsoft Word uses, so e.g., 3.5 and well-known count as one word each, and a dash standing on its own is not counted. Chinese and Japanese are written without spaces, so each of their characters counts as one word."
  - q: "Does it count spaces as characters?"
    a: "Both numbers are shown. Characters includes spaces and line breaks, which is how X, SMS and most forms count a limit. Characters without spaces leaves out every space, tab and line break, which some essay guidelines ask for."
  - q: "How is reading time worked out?"
    a: "Word count divided by a reading speed. The default is 238 words a minute, the average silent reading rate for English non-fiction found in a 2019 review of 190 studies by Marc Brysbaert. Fiction is read a little faster and technical text slower, so change the speed if you know your readers."
  - q: "Why does the SMS line say Unicode and fewer characters per message?"
    a: "A text message holds 160 characters only when every character is in the basic GSM alphabet. One character outside it, such as a Hindi letter, most emoji or a curly quote pasted from a word processor, switches the whole message to Unicode, which holds 70 characters, or 67 per part when the message is split."
  - q: "Is my text saved or sent anywhere?"
    a: "No. The counting runs in your browser as you type. The text is not uploaded, not stored and not shared with the analytics on this site. Close or reload the tab and it is gone. Only the reading speeds you set are remembered on your device."
  - q: "Why does X show a different count from this page?"
    a: "X weighs characters. Every link counts as 23 characters whatever its length, and Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters and many emoji count as two. This page counts each character once, so for a post close to 280 characters, check the count X shows before posting."
  - q: "Can I count words in a PDF or a Word file?"
    a: "Open the file, select all the text, copy it and paste it into the box. Page numbers, headers and footnotes come along with the paste, so the total can be a little higher than the body text alone."
